  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days...\n\nIn true summertime fashion we continue to have our weather\ndominated by the upper level ridge centered over the Great Basin.\nThis is competing with a weak upper level Hudson Bay Low which\nwill allow for weak disturbances to ride along the convergence of\nthese two just to the east of us. One of these weak features is \npassing to our east now which is dry but will bring a wind shift\nof a a more northerly component to the eastern 2/3 of the CWA. \nBehind this FROPA there could be a slightly higher concentration \nof wildfire smoke at the surface, but nothing of any concern.\n\nTomorrow, temperatures will continue to climb across the region \nwhere most locations will be in the low 90s. Additionally, the \nmoisture across the area will be quite limited resulting in Min RH\nvalues in the teens to single digits. This will create elevated \nfire weather concerns region-wide. There is a good signal of yet \nanother dry cold frontal passage for the eastern half of the CWA \ntomorrow afternoon into the evening. Winds with this FROPA could \ngust up to 30 mph in the plains creating locally critical fire \nweather concerns. Due to this brief window of critical fire \nweather, there is a Red Flag Warning in effect from 11am through \n8pm Thursday including Miles City and Baker. \n\nThe steady upward climb of temperatures continues into Friday.\nEnsembles have started to back off on the temperatures for the\nentire weekend through resulting in highs Saturday topping out in\nthe upper 90s for most; whereas the forecast previously looked \nlike widespread low 100s. Even if what the models are picking up \non, most likely the repeated shortwave activity, does not verify,\nit still does look likely that temps will struggle to reach 100 \nfor many. If the ridge does build back in slightly this would \nresult in dominant westerly winds throug much of the column which \nwould advect wildfire smoke from the PNW over the CWA which would \nkeep temps down, even with just a moderate concentration of \nvertically integrated smoke. \n\nSpeaking of this smoke it does look like that much of our area\nwill be spared of any high concentrations at the surface due to \nthe weaker, and more west centered, ridge. This would scenario \nwould likely result in easterly winds during the day due to the \nsurface thermal trough centered to the west in addition to simple\ndaytime upslope across the high plains. If the stronger ridge \nscenario winds out and more smoke is advected over the area this \nweekend, it would then be harder for there to be any meaningful \nsubsidence, in theory keeping the smoke aloft. Regardless, with \nthi much smoke over the the NW CONUS brief periods of bad air \nquality are always possible. \n\nAlthough the strength of the ridge into the weekend is stil \nminor question, models are in decent agreement in the ridge \nbuilding over our area for the strat of the work week. This will \nresult in continued slightly above average temperatures, hazy \nskies, dry conditions, and a potential return of weak monsoonal \nconvection. Although there is a lack of agreement how things \nshape up for the middle to end of next week, there is a signal in \nmost models that there could be some sort of low pressure system \nthat breaks down the ridge and potentially gives us some active \nweather. WMR",
